WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:09.800 align:start position:19%
Half a million books gone in a single
night?

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:14.800 align:start position:19%
Not stolen, not hidden.
Burned to ash while the greatest minds

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:18.720 align:start position:19%
on Earth watched helpless.
This is the story of the greatest

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:23.760 align:start position:19%
library ever built. And the day
humanity's memory went up in flames. I

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:28.320 align:start position:19%
am standing on the corniche of
Alexandria, Egypt, watching fishing

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:34.200 align:start position:19%
boats rock in the same harbor where
warships once docked.

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:39.720 align:start position:19%
Rises a giant tilted disc of glass and
granite, the new library of Alexandria,

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:44.120 align:start position:19%
opened in 2002.
The building it honors, that one

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:49.120 align:start position:19%
vanished 2,000 years ago.
I am Professor Adrian, and welcome back

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:53.280 align:start position:19%
to Lost Empires. Before we travel back
in time, I need to ask you something.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:58.720 align:start position:19%
The fire is coming, and you can save
exactly one scroll from the Great

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:01.760 align:start position:19%
Library. Just one. A lost play, a map of
the stars?

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:06.960 align:start position:19%
Tell me in the comments. I read every
single one, and your answer says a lot

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:11.800 align:start position:19%
about you. Now rewind. The year is 331
BC. A 25-year-old conqueror named

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:16.240 align:start position:19%
Alexander the Great stands on a strip of
land between the sea and a salty lake,

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:20.320 align:start position:19%
and points at the empty shore.
"Build my city here," he says. His

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:25.480 align:start position:19%
architect draws the street plan in
barley flour because there is no chalk.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:30.520 align:start position:19%
Birds swoop down and eat the flour, and
the priests call it a good omen. This

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:36.000 align:start position:19%
city will feed the world. Alexander dies
at 32, but his general Ptolemy seizes

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:41.240 align:start position:19%
Egypt and has a wild idea.
What if one city collected every book in

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:44.440 align:start position:19%
the world? Not the best books, all of
them.

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:49.240 align:start position:19%
He invites the scholar Demetrius, and
together they dream up the Mouseion, the

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:55.200 align:start position:19%
temple of the Muses, a research
institute with lecture halls, gardens, a

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:58.360 align:start position:19%
zoo, and at its heart the Great Library.
And here is the part that still gives me

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:03.360 align:start position:19%
chills.
Ptolemy does not wait for books to

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:08.720 align:start position:19%
arrive. He hunts them. Every ship
docking at Alexandria is searched. Every

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:14.760 align:start position:19%
scroll on board is seized, copied by an
army of scribes, and only the copy goes

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:19.880 align:start position:19%
back to the owner. The original stays.
The librarians were, technically, the

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:25.320 align:start position:19%
world's first copyright pirates. And
honestly, I love them for it. Within

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:30.520 align:start position:19%
decades, the shelves hold hundreds of
thousands of scrolls. Standing here

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:35.600 align:start position:19%
2,000 years ago, you would see porters
hauling cedar chests up the marble

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:39.880 align:start position:19%
steps, scribes bent over copying tables
in the courtyards, scholars arguing

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:45.400 align:start position:19%
under the colonnades in a dozen
languages. The smell of papyrus dust and

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:50.600 align:start position:19%
sea salt. It must have felt like
standing inside the brain of the world.

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:55.840 align:start position:19%
The librarians were not just collectors,
they were inventors. Callimachus of

00:02:53.200 --> 00:03:02.280 align:start position:19%
Cyrene builds the Pinakes, the first
library catalog in history. 120 volumes

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:06.959 align:start position:19%
listing every author and every work,
each with a short biography. It is the

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:11.959 align:start position:19%
ancestor of every search engine you have
ever used. When you type into Google,

00:03:09.959 --> 00:03:15.160 align:start position:19%
you are using an idea born on these
steps.

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:20.160 align:start position:19%
Then comes Eratosthenes, the head
librarian, and he attempts something

00:03:17.239 --> 00:03:25.000 align:start position:19%
absurd. He hears that at noon on one
summer day, the sun shines straight down

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:30.560 align:start position:19%
a deep well in a southern city, casting
no shadow. In Alexandria, there is a

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:37.040 align:start position:19%
shadow. With a stick, a shadow, and
geometry, he calculates the size of the

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:41.760 align:start position:19%
entire Earth and misses by barely 1%
2,000 years before satellites. In

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:46.239 align:start position:19%
another wing, doctors are mapping the
human body for the first time.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:51.000 align:start position:19%
Herophilus describes the brain, the
nerves, the pulse, and the vocabulary of

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:55.280 align:start position:19%
medicine is being written here on
papyrus in rooms smelling of ink and oil

00:03:53.960 --> 00:04:00.080 align:start position:19%
lamps.
Meanwhile, 72 scholars shut themselves

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:05.240 align:start position:19%
away to translate Hebrew scripture into
Greek, the Septuagint, the Bible most of

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:09.600 align:start position:19%
the ancient world would read.
&gt;&gt; Hi, I'm Yunus. Welcome to Ancient

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:14.720 align:start position:19%
Empires.
&gt;&gt; The Ptolemies paid scholars a salary

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:20.640 align:start position:19%
just to think. No classes to teach, no
forms to fill, free meals, a bed, and

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:26.200 align:start position:19%
one job: be curious.
For 600 years, Alexandria runs the most

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:30.280 align:start position:19%
radical experiment in history.
What happens when you pay brilliant

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:35.919 align:start position:19%
people simply to wonder? If you love
stories like this, lost worlds and real

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:40.760 align:start position:19%
history with no boring textbook voice,
hit subscribe because Lost Empires

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:44.240 align:start position:19%
brings you a new vanished civilization
every single day.

00:04:42.919 --> 00:04:49.760 align:start position:19%
Now, the fire.
Because every golden age has a night

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:54.440 align:start position:19%
when the sky turns orange. The year is
48 BC.

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:59.360 align:start position:19%
Julius Caesar is trapped in Alexandria,
outnumbered, fighting the army of the

00:04:56.520 --> 00:05:04.400 align:start position:19%
boy king Ptolemy the 13th.
To save his fleet, Caesar orders his men

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:08.919 align:start position:19%
to torch the enemy ships in the harbor.
The wind does the rest.

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:12.640 align:start position:19%
Flames leap from hull to hull, then to
the dockside warehouses.

00:05:10.919 --> 00:05:18.120 align:start position:19%
And somewhere in the smoke, near the
water, stand the storehouses of books.

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:23.120 align:start position:19%
Picture it. Midnight.
The harbor glowing red.

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:28.280 align:start position:19%
Scrolls, hundreds of thousands of them,
curling black in the heat. Their words

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:33.160 align:start position:19%
lifting into the sky as sparks.
Scholars wading into the shallows, arms

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:36.160 align:start position:19%
full of papyrus, saving what they can
carry.

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:41.040 align:start position:19%
Nobody that night knows they are
watching the beginning of the end.

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:45.680 align:start position:19%
But here is what the movies never tell
you. The library did not die in one

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:50.880 align:start position:19%
night. The main collection probably
survived Caesar's fire. It stood in the

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:54.840 align:start position:19%
palace quarter, uphill from the docks.
What killed it was slower. Wars, budget

00:05:53.880 --> 00:06:00.360 align:start position:19%
cuts.
The emperor Aurelian sacks the quarter

00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:05.520 align:start position:19%
in 270 AD. A Christian mob destroys the
daughter library in 391.

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:10.280 align:start position:19%
The history loves a single villain. The
truth is slower and sadder. Consider

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:15.520 align:start position:19%
what faded with it.
We own seven plays by Sophocles. He

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:20.400 align:start position:19%
wrote 123.
Of Sappho's nine books of poetry, we

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:25.120 align:start position:19%
have scraps that fit in your palm.
Complete histories, star charts, medical

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:30.120 align:start position:19%
texts, gone.
Not burned in one dramatic night, but

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:35.720 align:start position:19%
crumbling century by century because
nobody copied them anymore. What amazes

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:40.720 align:start position:19%
me is this. A single surviving scroll
could rewrite your school textbooks.

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:45.560 align:start position:19%
Imagine unrolling a papyrus and finding
a map of the Atlantic drawn a thousand

00:06:42.919 --> 00:06:50.919 align:start position:19%
years before Columbus. Or a comedy
funnier than anything on stage today.

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:55.320 align:start position:19%
Somewhere under modern Alexandria, in
the mud and ground water, those scrolls

00:06:53.480 --> 00:07:00.440 align:start position:19%
may still be waiting.
Papyrus can survive millennia in the

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:05.840 align:start position:19%
right mud. And then there is Cleopatra,
the last great mind of Alexandria.

00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:10.080 align:start position:19%
She speaks nine languages. She funds the
scholars. She is the library's final

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:15.560 align:start position:19%
patron.
When she and Mark Antony lose to Rome,

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:20.200 align:start position:19%
the city's golden age ends with her.
I always picture her in the reading

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:24.919 align:start position:19%
rooms at dusk. Lamplight on the scrolls,
knowing the world she loves is about to

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:29.440 align:start position:19%
change forever. Here is the mystery that
keeps archaeologists awake at night.

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:34.040 align:start position:19%
We still do not know exactly where the
great library stood. The palace quarter

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:39.760 align:start position:19%
sank. Earthquakes dropped it into the
harbor. Today, divers swim over sunken

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:44.280 align:start position:19%
streets, past sphinxes and columns lying
in the sand. The greatest library in

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:48.680 align:start position:19%
history may sit under 20 ft of
Mediterranean water, and we walk above

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:52.800 align:start position:19%
it every day.
The digs keep the hope alive.

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:57.720 align:start position:19%
At Kom el-Dikka, archaeologists
uncovered ancient lecture halls with

00:07:54.680 --> 00:08:01.760 align:start position:19%
curved stone benches. 13 rooms where
students once sat.

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:07.400 align:start position:19%
You can stand there today, run your hand
along the same limestone, and hear the

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:10.880 align:start position:19%
guide say, "This was a university 1,800
years ago."

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:14.760 align:start position:19%
The shovels have not stopped. Neither
has the dream.

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:18.840 align:start position:19%
Every few years, a diver surfaces near
the old harbor holding another statue,

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:21.800 align:start position:19%
another inscription.
Each one is a sentence recovered from

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:27.600 align:start position:19%
the fire.
It is slow, muddy, unglamorous work, and

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:33.320 align:start position:19%
it is the most romantic thing I know.
Because every stone they lift proves

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:38.120 align:start position:19%
that forgetting is not forever. In 2002,
Egypt opened the Bibliotheca

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:42.960 align:start position:19%
Alexandrina, a new library on the same
shore shaped like a rising sun. Its

00:08:40.840 --> 00:08:48.480 align:start position:19%
granite walls carved with letters from
every alphabet on Earth.

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:53.520 align:start position:19%
Inside, 8 million books and servers
archiving the entire internet.

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:58.680 align:start position:19%
The message is carved in stone. We will
not lose it all again.

00:08:55.839 --> 00:09:03.680 align:start position:19%
So, what is the lesson of Alexandria?
Not only that fire destroys, it is that

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:09.040 align:start position:19%
memory is a choice.
Every generation decides what to copy,

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:14.200 align:start position:19%
what to fund, what to remember.
The ancients chose to gather everything,

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:18.480 align:start position:19%
and for six centuries, it worked.
We have better tools than papyrus now.

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:21.800 align:start position:19%
The question is whether we have the same
will.

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:25.839 align:start position:19%
If the fire of Alexandria moved you,
wait until you see what a volcano did to

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:31.080 align:start position:19%
an entire Roman city in a single
morning. Watch our Pompeii story next.

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:35.640 align:start position:19%
The link is on screen now. Subscribe to
Lost Empires, and tomorrow we open

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:41.960 align:start position:19%
another lost world together. Because the
past is never really gone. It is just

00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:41.960 align:start position:19%
waiting for someone to remember it.

